{"step_index":1399,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-20T08:10:37Z","input_tokens":2412,"cache_read_tokens":177654,"output_tokens":1261,"thinking":"The `/learn` command indicates the user wants to preserve knowledge gained from the interaction. This suggests a significant insight, protocol, workflow, setup procedure, or behavioral nuance has been established and should be retained for future tasks. I will now focus on capturing the relevant context from the prior conversation to encode this new knowledge.\n\n\nThe user wants to preserve the knowledge established during the interaction, specifically regarding the complete state transfer protocol for AIMD simulations. The identified issue is that standard LAMMPS restarts don't preserve the internal state of the Nosé-Hoover thermostat, causing temperature discontinuities. The solution involves retaining past QE output and using an MDI engine to instantly return cached energies and forces to LAMMPS. This ensures a complete state restoration, including the thermostat's internal variables, at the restart point, while also handling the transfer of converged wavefunctions and charge densities from Quantum ESPRESSO.\n\n\nThe simulation will restart using a 'medium' checkpoint and seamlessly transition to an actual QE GPU SCF calculation from the new step. Regular `write_restart` operations will also be used as a safeguard.\n\nInitial AIMD from a 0 K perfect crystal to 300 K results in an energy drop due to equipartition, causing an initial temperature dip. The first 100 steps are a transient equilibration phase given the Nosé-Hoover thermostat's time constant (100 fs). Ensemble averages of potential energy and temperature must be calculated from the later stages, specifically after 300 steps.\n\nThe Winmostar 3D viewer blanks out due to extreme zoom levels leading to off-screen panning; the solution is to reset the view with the `Ctrl + F` shortcut.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"